\title{Margin comments}
\import{base-macros}
\tag{feature-demo}
\tag{showcase}
\meta{venue}{Feature gallery}

\p{Some remarks belong neither in the running text nor in a footnote at the very
bottom of the page — you want them \comment{right here}{An aside sits in the
margin, level with the text it annotates, rather than collected at the foot of
the page.}, in the margin, tied to a specific phrase. The \code{comment} macro
does exactly that: \code{\startverb\comment{anchored phrase}{the note}\stopverb}
underlines the phrase and lifts the note into the right margin, joined to it by a
thin line that runs only through the gutter, never across the prose.}

\p{Asides are \comment{spatially owned}{Each paragraph's comments live in the
vertical band beside it, so one paragraph's notes never shove the next
paragraph's around.} by the paragraph they annotate. When a single paragraph
gathers \comment{more}{First.} notes \comment{than}{Second.} will comfortably
\comment{fit}{Third.} beside \comment{it}{Fourth.}, the extras fold into a small
"+N more" you can expand — and hovering any underline pops its own note out with
a connecting line, so nothing is ever truly out of reach.}

\subtree[0084]{
  \taxon{Definition}
  \title{some cool term}
  
  \p{Definition text...}
}

\p{On a narrow screen there is no margin to speak of, so the underline becomes
\comment{tap-to-reveal}{The note opens inline, just beneath the paragraph — the
footnote-pop-in fallback that Tufte CSS and Gwern's sidenotes settle on for
mobile.}: tap it and the note expands inline beneath the paragraph.}
